claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> Reading goes through a local copy of lists/ that only grows. Clients
> can never change Fastmail, reading keeps working while Fastmail is
> down, and deleting mail on Fastmail loses nothing. Clients use IMAP
> rather than a shared mount, so agents need not run on beastie.

"that only grows" is awkward. Better to describe it as "append-only."

> +sending:
> +- Software: smtprelay (mail/smtprelay).
> +- smtprelay does not queue. It relays each message to Fastmail SMTP
> +  during the client's session, logging in with the stored
> +  credentials, and answers the client's DATA with Fastmail's reply.
> +  When Fastmail rejects a message, the client gets Fastmail's code
> +  and text. When Fastmail cannot be reached, the client gets a 4xx
> +  reply. smtprelay never accepts a message it has not handed to
> +  Fastmail, and never sends a bounce.

Interesting, so this is the tradeoff I guess? With a queueing server, I
can deliver emails from the client offline.

How does this scenario work when I can't reach the remote server? I
anticipate notmuch-emacs will be my primary client for working with this
system.

> +  Fastmail, or moved out of it, stays in the local maildir. Fastmail
> +  is the inbox, and the local maildir only grows.

I think it's better to call it append-only rather than only-grows.

> +clients: lab-claude.
> +- Packages: mail/isync and mail/msmtp, added to the existing
> +  mail/notmuch.
> +- msmtp sends through 127.0.0.1:2525 as claude@p7x.dev. When the
> +  server rejects any recipient, msmtp sends no DATA and exits non-zero
> +  with the server's reply, so the whole message is abandoned.

How does notmuch-email respond in this case?
